begin;

select public.seed_cipmn_mock_module('CIPMN-MOD-009','[["Agile mindset and customer value","The team completes many tasks and documents, but customers receive no usable improvement and feedback is postponed until the end.","Reorient delivery toward frequent usable value, customer feedback and learning.","Measure progress only by hours worked and documents produced.","Prevent customers from seeing work until every feature is finished.","Increase the number of simultaneous tasks without changing delivery.","Agile emphasises customer value, working solutions, collaboration and adaptive learning rather than activity volume."],["Responding to change","A validated regulatory change makes part of the original backlog obsolete, but the team insists on delivering the old plan exactly.","Assess the change, reprioritise transparently and adapt the plan while protecting the product goal.","Ignore the regulation because the original plan was approved.","Cancel all current work without analysing impact.","Hide the change until the Sprint Review.","Agile welcomes valuable change, but adaptation should remain transparent and aligned with goals and constraints."],["Iterative and incremental delivery","A service is divided into small components, but the team plans to build all components partially and integrate them only at the end.","Deliver complete, integrated, usable increments that can be inspected and improved.","Maximise partially finished work across all components.","Call every internal task an increment even when it creates no usable outcome.","Delay integration and testing until the final release.","An increment should be integrated and usable; iteration enables refinement based on feedback."],["Agile versus predictive fit","A fixed civil-works component has stable design approvals, while a citizen-facing digital component has uncertain requirements and needs frequent feedback.","Use a tailored hybrid approach, managing stable work predictively and uncertain product work iteratively.","Force both components into Scrum because Agile is always superior.","Force both components into Waterfall because construction is involved.","Run the components independently without integrated milestones or governance.","Delivery approaches should match uncertainty, dependency and governance needs; hybrid delivery may be appropriate."],["Agile cultural shift and empowerment","Managers assign every task, approve every minor decision and punish teams for raising problems, while expecting Agile speed.","Create empowered cross-functional teams, transparent information, psychological safety and clear decision boundaries.","Add more daily approvals to improve control.","Rename managers as Scrum Masters without changing behaviour.","Discourage escalation so the team appears self-organising.","Agile requires trust, collaboration, transparency and delegated decision-making, not command-and-control with new labels."],["Scrum accountabilities","Stakeholders give conflicting priorities directly to Developers, and no one is accountable for maximising product value.","Empower the Product Owner to order the Product Backlog and provide a single transparent priority direction.","Allow every stakeholder to reorder work during the Sprint.","Ask the Scrum Master to own product value and approve features.","Let Developers choose priorities solely by technical interest.","The Product Owner is accountable for maximising product value and effective Product Backlog management."],["Scrum Master role","The team has recurring impediments and weak Scrum practice, but the Scrum Master spends the Sprint assigning tasks and rating individual performance.","Coach the team and organisation, facilitate Scrum effectiveness and help remove impediments without becoming a command-and-control manager.","Continue assigning all work because the Scrum Master is the team boss.","Replace the Product Owner and set product priorities.","Perform all difficult development tasks personally.","The Scrum Master serves Scrum adoption and team effectiveness, facilitates and coaches, and is not a traditional task manager."],["Scrum events","The Daily Scrum becomes a one-hour status presentation to senior management and produces no plan for the next day.","Refocus it as a brief Developers'' event to inspect progress toward the Sprint Goal and adapt the plan.","Extend it so every stakeholder can present a report.","Use it to reprioritise the Product Backlog for future releases.","Cancel it because collaboration should occur only at Sprint Review.","The Daily Scrum is a timeboxed planning and inspection event for Developers focused on the Sprint Goal."],["Definition of Done and increment quality","A team reports stories as complete even though integration, security testing and documentation required for release are unfinished.","Apply a transparent Definition of Done and count only work meeting it as part of the increment.","Mark stories done when coding starts so velocity improves.","Use a different hidden definition for each team member.","Move unfinished quality work to a future project without disclosure.","The Definition of Done creates shared quality transparency and prevents incomplete work from being represented as usable value."],["Kanban work-in-progress limits","The team starts new requests whenever someone is free, resulting in many blocked items and long cycle times.","Visualise flow, enforce work-in-progress limits and help finish blocked or active items before starting more.","Increase the number of open items so everyone remains busy.","Remove blocked work from the board.","Measure productivity by how many items are started.","WIP limits expose bottlenecks, reduce multitasking and improve flow and completion."],["Agile planning levels","Executives ask for direction, release expectations and Sprint detail, but the team maintains only a daily task list.","Connect product vision and roadmap to release planning, Product Backlog ordering and Sprint planning.","Use the daily task list as the product strategy.","Create a fixed detailed plan for every task several years ahead.","Plan only at executive level and let Sprints begin without goals.","Agile planning occurs at multiple connected horizons, with detail increasing as work approaches."],["Agile estimation and story points","Management treats story points as hours and compares individual Developers'' productivity across teams.","Use story points as a team-relative estimate of effort, complexity and uncertainty, not as individual time or performance targets.","Convert every point to exactly one hour.","Use higher point totals as evidence that one person works harder.","Set estimates after completion so velocity appears stable.","Story points are relative team estimates and should support planning, not individual appraisal or cross-team productivity comparison."],["User stories and acceptance criteria","A backlog item says only ''build dashboard'' and provides no user, need, value or acceptance conditions.","Refine it into a clear user-centred story with testable acceptance criteria.","Keep it broad so any output can be accepted.","Replace it with a list of developer tasks only.","Define acceptance after the Sprint ends.","Effective user stories communicate who needs what and why, while acceptance criteria clarify expected behaviour."],["User story mapping and thin slices","The backlog is a flat list of hundreds of items and the team cannot identify an end-to-end minimum useful release.","Use story mapping to visualise the user journey, prioritise outcomes and select thin vertical slices for release.","Build all database components before any user-facing flow.","Order stories only by when they were written.","Select the largest stories first to maximise utilisation.","Story mapping connects work to the user journey and supports coherent incremental releases."],["Backlog refinement and prioritisation","The Product Backlog contains outdated, oversized and unestimated items, and Sprint Planning is repeatedly delayed.","Refine the backlog continuously and order items using value, risk, urgency, dependency and learning considerations.","Refine the backlog only once at project initiation.","Mark every item as highest priority.","Let the loudest stakeholder select Sprint work without Product Owner accountability.","An effective backlog is transparent, ordered and sufficiently refined for near-term planning while remaining adaptable."]]'::jsonb);

commit;
